DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'MathObjects.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');
TEXT(beginproblem());
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;
$prompt = <<'PROMPT_3D38_6BF8';
<p>3d38_6bf8</p> <table border="0" style="border: 0px solid white; border-collapse: collapse; "><colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <tr> <td></td> <td align="center">Gene 1</td> <td></td> <td align="center">Gene 2</td></tr><tr> <td align="center" style="background-color: cornflowerblue">blue</td> <td align="center">&xrarr;</td> <td align="center" style="background-color: powderblue">sky blue</td> <td align="center">&xrarr;</td> <td align="center" style="background-color: ivory">white</td></tr></table><br/>A two step metabolic pathway determines the pigment color. When gene 1 is dominant, the gene expresses an enzyme that converts the blue precursor pigment into the sky blue pigment. When gene 1 is homozygous recessive, there is no pigment change and the blue precursor pigment remains. Gene 2 only has an effect on the pigment when gene 1 is dominant. When gene 2 is dominant, the gene expresses an enzyme that converts the sky blue pigment into the white pigment. <br/> <br/><p>The diagram and description above explain the interaction of two genes. <b>Determine the dihybird cross phenotypic ratio.</b></p>
PROMPT_3D38_6BF8
